+# Agent Note: Page-preserving PDF text in web fetch
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-27-pdf-web-fetch.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+`web_fetch` accepts public HTTP(S) resources but rejects `application/pdf`, even when a document contains directly extractable text. Returning PDF bytes as text would produce unusable model input, while parsing a prefix after the response byte cap would treat an incomplete document as valid. Flattening every page into one string would also discard page references that users, models, and source documents rely on.
+
+PDF support must remain bounded by the existing fetch timeout, abort signal, response byte cap, and decoded-character cap. It also needs a page limit because a small file can declare many pages. Scanned or image-only documents require OCR, which adds image rendering, language models or native OCR engines, larger dependencies, and nondeterministic output beyond deterministic URL retrieval.
+
+## Decision
+
+Within the [web capability seam](../architecture/2026-06-24-web-capability-seam.md), `WebFetchBody` is a closed union with a `pdf` arm containing `pages: readonly string[]` and `pageCount`. The provider retains one page slot per source page, including empty pages, so the array index plus one is the stable page number and `pageCount` always equals `pages.length`.
+
+`dsh-web-fetch-http` classifies normalized `application/pdf` responses and advertises PDF support in `Accept`. It reads the complete response under `maxResponseBytes` before parsing; declared or streaming overflow fails with `WEB_FETCH_TOO_LARGE`, and no truncated PDF is passed to the parser.
+
+The provider loads `pdfjs-dist` lazily only for PDF responses. It disables system fonts, WebAssembly, and worker fetches, uses the dependency's packaged CMaps and standard fonts, extracts pages in source order, and normalizes whitespace and explicit line endings deterministically. Parsing shares the fetch signal and deadline, and the loading task is destroyed before the fetch settles.
+
+`maxPdfPages` is a validated positive-integer provider setting with a default of `100`. A document over that limit fails with `WEB_PDF_TOO_MANY_PAGES`. The existing `maxBodyChars` cap applies across ordered page text while retaining all page slots and setting `truncated`.
+
+Malformed documents fail with `WEB_PDF_MALFORMED`, encrypted or password-protected documents with `WEB_PDF_PASSWORD_REQUIRED`, and documents with no extractable text with `WEB_PDF_NO_EXTRACTABLE_TEXT`. Timeout and caller cancellation retain `WEB_FETCH_TIMEOUT` and `WEB_ABORTED`. The provider never performs OCR.
+
+`dsh-tool-web` renders `PDF pages: <count>` followed by `--- Page <number> of <count> ---` sections. Its complete-output cap may omit later sections or cut page text, but it never cuts through a page marker when the cap leaves room for the normal truncation footer.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Keep PDF unsupported.** Rejected because public reports and reference documents frequently expose their primary text only as PDF, and the existing local fetch operation can decode them without a provider-backed extraction service.
+
+**Run OCR for image-only pages.** Rejected because OCR introduces a separate image-processing capability, platform dependencies, language configuration, and output variability. Image-only PDFs fail explicitly so a future OCR capability can define those requirements independently.
+
+**Return one flattened PDF string.** Rejected because page boundaries are stable source coordinates and cost little to preserve in the closed result type.
+
+**Parse bytes collected up to the response cap.** Rejected because a truncated PDF is not a valid partial text response. The provider fails before parsing when the complete resource exceeds the byte limit.
+
+## Verification
+
+Deterministic fixtures cover multi-page text, MIME parameters, same-origin redirects, malformed, encrypted, image-only, oversized, over-page-limit, character-capped, timed-out, and aborted PDFs. The recorded `web-fetch` Session runs the shipped headless profile against a loopback PDF fixture and pins the model-visible page markers, prompt guidance, and tool schema.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Text-bearing PDFs become ordinary `web_fetch` results with page-aware model output and the same public-network policy as HTML and text. `pdfjs-dist` and its optional platform packages increase install size, but lazy loading keeps non-PDF fetch startup unchanged. PDF extraction remains best-effort for embedded text: layout, tables, reading order, and fonts are limited by the source document and PDF.js, while OCR remains unavailable.
